  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft with a lead shortwave lifting from near\nthe MT border through the Northern Plains. Storms have been\nongoing in northeastern ND into far northwestern MN, and will\ncontinue to push eastward along the warm front/theta-e gradient\nthat is between the Highway 2 and Highway 200 corridors. The\nshortwave will move northeast overnight, and upper ridging will\nbegin to amplify over the Plains tomorrow. Upper ridging\ncontinues to build over the region into the weekend, with\nseveral weak embedded shortwaves possible. By early next week,\nthe upper ridge starts to flatten and move east, with continued\nactive weather under southwesterly flow aloft. A trough moving\nthrough Manitoba and Ontario on Wednesday will push east\nThursday and leave the region in northwesterly flow.\n\n...Convective trends and severe threat this evening...\n\nComplex of thunderstorms in northeastern ND is currently\ncrossing into northwestern MN. Most of the instability seems to\nbe elevated, with SPC mesoanalysis still showing a good amount \nof CIN on surface based and mixed layer CAPE, but there has been\nenough mixing to get a few 60 and even 70 mph reports to the \nsurface. The shear at 0-3km is favorable for brief tornado spin\nups along some kinks in the line, but would need to get some\nbetter low level CAPE developing before that could happen. Think\nthat this will be the case eventually with sunshine over the\nsouthern forecast area. Even if we don't get a fully mixed\nboundary layer, with continued lift, good deep layer shear \naround 50 kts, and dew points in the upper 60s to near 70\nfeeding into the complex, expect some re-intensification of\nstorms and a continued threat of large hail and winds up to 70\nmph. \n\n...Storm chances Saturday and Sunday...\n\nAfter a break tomorrow, additional weak embedded shortwaves\nunder the building ridge will bring some rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms. Temperatures will be well above average for early\nSeptember and there should be good instability to work with. The\ndeep layer shear is not as strong this weekend as today, but at\n30 kts could still be adequate for strong to severe storms.\nMachine learning has some low probabilities for severe impacts\nfor the weekend time period, mainly in the 5 to 15 percent\nrange. Still some uncertainty, but will have to watch for some\nactive convective days this holiday weekend.",
